# Deployed scripts must stay LF-only regardless of the authoring OS/git
# config -- see TODO.md's "Deployment bugs found in production" section:
# a Windows-side commit with core.autocrlf=true previously reintroduced
# CRLF line endings into these files, breaking them on the Linux/Apache
# deployment target until manually fixed with `sed -i 's/\r$//'`.
#
# `src/html/**/*.py` (not the old, silently-broken `html/**/*.py`): a
# gitattributes pattern containing a slash anchors to the *repository
# root*, and every CGI script this is meant to protect actually lives
# under `src/html/` (the "src layout" -- see setup.py's own comment on
# it), not a top-level `html/`. Confirmed the old pattern matched nothing
# at all (`git check-attr eol -- src/html/sector.py` came back
# "unspecified") -- this rule has been silently inert since whenever the
# src-layout move happened, the exact CRLF-corruption class it exists to
# prevent left unprotected the whole time.
#
# `*.sh` (not just `examples/apache/*.sh`): install.sh/update.sh at the
# repo root are just as "deployed" and depended-on-being-executable as
# examples/apache/set-permissions.sh, and were likewise left out by the
# old, narrower pattern -- one repo-wide rule instead, mirroring how
# install.sh's own step 4 already treats "every *.sh in the repo" alike
# (`find "$SCRIPT_DIR" -name '*.sh'`) rather than a hardcoded list.
src/html/**/*.py text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# Vendored third-party build output (src/html/static/vendor/, see that
# directory's THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt) -- `binary` (implies `-text
# -diff -merge`) keeps it byte-for-byte identical to what was actually
# built/committed regardless of the checking-out OS/git config, the same
# CRLF-corruption class the rules above guard against for deployed
# scripts. A minified bundle also has no meaningful line-based diff to
# preserve in the first place.
src/html/static/vendor/* binary
